<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhar Tichitt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tichitt-culture">Tichitt</a> is an escarpment in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> with extensive remains of stone-built settlements. The Tichitt culture, traceable to around 1600 BCE, is regarded by the site as the archaeological forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> people who built it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassaniya-arabic">Hassaniya Arabic</a> word dhar, an escarpment. The region is one of steep sandstone cliffs, sandy flats with scattered clumps of coarse grass, and depressions between the dunes. Today it is arid and hot, with about 100 millimetres of rain a year, but during the Holocene it was far more temperate, and in its humid phases small lakes formed between the dunes. Dhar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tichitt-culture">Tichitt</a>, which includes Dakhlet el Atrouss, is one of several escarpments of the Tichitt culture, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhar-walata">Dhar Walata</a>, Dhar Néma and Dhar Tagant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Patrick Munson first excavated the sites extensively in the late 1960s and published them in 1971, dividing them into eight phases; later work by Holl, Amblard and MacDonald reorganised this sequence, though the chronology is still contested. About 500 settlements are known in the region. Their people herded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and goats, hunted, fished, gathered wild grain and grew bulrush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, whose domestication was first shown by grain impressions on potsherds. Carnelian and amazonite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, stone arm rings and axes point to long-distance trade.</p><p>In the Classic Tichitt period, from 1600 to 1000 BCE, settlements grew into clusters of drystone compounds holding houses and granaries, some laid out along streets, with large livestock enclosures nearby. They fell into four tiers, from hamlets of 2 ha to regional centres of 80 ha, and Dhar Tichitt may have been the main centre. Its walls seem to have divided social space rather than kept out attackers, and its unusually many tumuli suggest a ritual importance.</p><p>In the Late Tichitt period, which began around 1000 BCE, aridification and population decline set in. Desertification and the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> groups are both seen as causes of the collapse, and the displaced inhabitants are thought to have moved gradually into the northern Middle Niger valley. Many Fulbe clans trace their origin to the area, which they called Maasina, the name later borne by the Massina Empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhar Tichitt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ghana Empire, known internally as Wagadou, is often linked to the Tichitt stone-settlement culture of Mauritania, traceable archaeologically to the second millennium BCE at <b>Dhar Tichitt</b> and Dhar Walata.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the last millennium BCE, agropastoralists at <b>Dhar Tichitt</b> in Mauritania were repeatedly raided from the north by people archaeologists think were Libyco-Berbers or Garamantes, perhaps taken as labourers to the Fezzan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/garamantes">Garamantes · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
